From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:01:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] patches/openwrt: fix powerpc32 eBPF JIT miscompilations

The powerpc32 eBPF JIT used by mpc85xx and apm821xx miscompiles two
kinds of programs.

Since 19daf0aef84f ("powerpc/bpf/32: perform three operands ALU
operations") in 6.3, a MOV X is merged into the following ALU operation
on the same register and its own code is dropped - also when that
operation uses the register as its source, as the verifier's
zero-extension does. Clang chains bpf_csum_diff() calls through such a
pair, so on a WS-AP3715i CLAT sent packets with bad L4 checksums as soon
as bpf_jit_enable was 1.

In BPF-to-BPF calls, the callee gets the upper half of BPF_REG_1 as its
first argument and returns only the lower half of BPF_REG_0 (both since
51c66ad849a7), and it doesn't receive BPF_REG_5 (since 7dd0e2848764).

Carry the fixes as pending kernel patches until they are upstream.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 ...x-powerpc32-eBPF-JIT-miscompilations.patch | 306 ++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 306 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 patches/openwrt/0023-kernel-fix-powerpc32-eBPF-JIT-miscompilations.patch

diff --git a/patches/openwrt/0023-kernel-fix-powerpc32-eBPF-JIT-miscompilations.patch b/patches/openwrt/0023-kernel-fix-powerpc32-eBPF-JIT-miscompilations.patch
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..4a9d85de
--- /dev/null
+++ b/patches/openwrt/0023-kernel-fix-powerpc32-eBPF-JIT-miscompilations.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,306 @@
+From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
+From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
+Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:01:25 +0000
+Subject: [PATCH] kernel: fix powerpc32 eBPF JIT miscompilations
+
+The powerpc32 eBPF JIT, used by mpc85xx and apm821xx, generates wrong
+code for BPF-to-BPF calls and for some MOVs followed by an ALU
+operation.
+
+A MOV X followed by an ALU operation on the same register is emitted as
+one three-operand operation, and the code for the MOV is dropped. Since
+19daf0aef84f ("powerpc/bpf/32: perform three operands ALU operations")
+in 6.3 this also happens when the second instruction uses the
+destination register as its source, which then still holds the value
+from before the MOV. The zero-extension the verifier inserts after a
+32-bit MOV is such an instruction. Clang passes the result of one
+bpf_csum_diff() to the next with "w5 = w0", which the verifier follows
+with a zero-extension, so on a WS-AP3715i every chained call got seed 0
+and CLAT sent packets with bad L4 checksums as soon as bpf_jit_enable
+was 1.
+
+BPF-to-BPF calls pass their arguments like helper calls, but the callee
+used the prologue and epilogue of a main program: it treated r3 as a
+32-bit context pointer, so it got the upper half of BPF_REG_1 as its
+first argument, and it returned only the lower half of BPF_REG_0, where
+the caller expects the upper half. Both date back to 51c66ad849a7
+("powerpc/bpf: Implement extended BPF on PPC32"). Since 7dd0e2848764
+("powerpc/bpf/32: BPF prog is never called with more than one arg") the
+callee also doesn't load BPF_REG_5 from the caller's parameter area.
+A program that passes its arguments on to a call or tail call without
+using them can also have those registers overwritten.
+
+Add the fixes as pending patches. They are backported from a series
+that is not upstream yet.
+
+Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
+---
+ ...fix-argument-passing-for-subprograms.patch | 91 +++++++++++++++++++
+ ...tch-fifth-argument-in-BPF-subprogram.patch | 32 +++++++
+ ...rk-all-argument-registers-seen-in-fu.patch | 69 ++++++++++++++
+ ...n-t-fold-MOV-into-instructions-that-.patch | 40 ++++++++
+ 4 files changed, 232 insertions(+)
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/343-powerpc32-bpf-fix-argument-passing-for-subprograms.patch
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/344-powerpc32-bpf-fetch-fifth-argument-in-BPF-subprogram.patch
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/345-powerpc32-bpf-mark-all-argument-registers-seen-in-fu.patch
+ create mode 100644 target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/346-powerpc32-bpf-don-t-fold-MOV-into-instructions-that-.patch
+
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/343-powerpc32-bpf-fix-argument-passing-for-subprograms.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/343-powerpc32-bpf-fix-argument-passing-for-subprograms.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..8695baf
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/343-powerpc32-bpf-fix-argument-passing-for-subprograms.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,91 @@
++From d7756fdcc4a81348e2e42a6031f6d2b0e8f82a26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:21:53 +0100
++Subject: [PATCH 1/4] powerpc32/bpf: fix argument passing for subprograms
++
++Main programs receive a single 32-bit pointer as the first argument,
++so the current prologue converts it to the 64-bit BPF_REG_1 by moving
++R3 to R4 and zeroing R3.
++
++However, subprograms are called like helper functions, so there the first
++argument already is 64-bit - but the prologue still does its conversion,
++so the argument ends up with its halves swapped and the upper one zeroed.
++The same problem applies to the return value.
++
++In particular, in the common case where the first argument is a context
++pointer, the callee ends up with the upper (zeroed) 32 bits, which then
++triggers a kernel panic as soon as it's used.
++
++Therefore, make JIT emit the appropriate 64-bit prologue/epilogue for
++subprograms.
++
++Fixes: 51c66ad849a7 ("powerpc/bpf: Implement extended BPF on PPC32")
++Signed-off-by: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++
++[ Backport to 6.12: struct codegen_context has no is_subprog yet. Add it
++  and set it from fp->aux->func_idx in bpf_int_jit_compile(); the extra
++  pass restores it from jit_data->ctx. ]
++---
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit.h        |  1 +
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp.c   |  1 +
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c | 16 ++++++++++++----
++ 3 files changed, 14 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)
++
++diff --git a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit.h b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit.h
++index cdea5dc..2795ccf 100644
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit.h
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit.h
++@@ -134,6 +134,7 @@ struct codegen_context {
++ 	int b2p[MAX_BPF_JIT_REG + 2];
++ 	unsigned int exentry_idx;
++ 	unsigned int alt_exit_addr;
+++	bool is_subprog;
++ };
++ 
++ #define bpf_to_ppc(r)	(ctx->b2p[r])
++diff --git a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp.c b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp.c
++index 180175e..f0063eb 100644
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp.c
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp.c
++@@ -134,6 +134,7 @@ struct bpf_prog *bpf_int_jit_compile(struct bpf_prog *fp)
++ 
++ 	/* Make sure that the stack is quadword aligned. */
++ 	cgctx.stack_size = round_up(fp->aux->stack_depth, 16);
+++	cgctx.is_subprog = fp->aux->func_idx != 0;
++ 
++ 	/* Scouting faux-generate pass 0 */
++ 	if (bpf_jit_build_body(fp, NULL, NULL, &cgctx, addrs, 0, false)) {
++diff --git a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
++index a0c4f1b..52d1182 100644
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
++@@ -141,9 +141,11 @@ void bpf_jit_build_prologue(u32 *image, struct codegen_context *ctx)
++ 	if (ctx->seen & SEEN_TAILCALL)
++ 		EMIT(PPC_RAW_STW(_R4, _R1, bpf_jit_stack_offsetof(ctx, BPF_PPC_TC)));
++ 
++-	/* First arg comes in as a 32 bits pointer. */
++-	EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1), _R3));
++-	EMIT(PPC_RAW_LI(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1) - 1, 0));
+++	/* Programs receive the first arg as a 32 bits pointer, subprograms don't. */
+++	if (!ctx->is_subprog) {
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1), _R3));
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_LI(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1) - 1, 0));
+++	}
++ 
++ 	/*
++ 	 * We need a stack frame, but we don't necessarily need to
++@@ -193,7 +195,13 @@ static void bpf_jit_emit_common_epilogue(u32 *image, struct codegen_context *ctx
++ 
++ void bpf_jit_build_epilogue(u32 *image, struct codegen_context *ctx)
++ {
++-	EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(_R3, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_0)));
+++	/* Programs return a 32-bit value, while subprograms return 64 bit. */
+++	if (ctx->is_subprog) {
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(_R3, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_0) - 1));
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(_R4, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_0)));
+++	} else {
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_MR(_R3, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_0)));
+++	}
++ 
++ 	bpf_jit_emit_common_epilogue(image, ctx);
++ 
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/344-powerpc32-bpf-fetch-fifth-argument-in-BPF-subprogram.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/344-powerpc32-bpf-fetch-fifth-argument-in-BPF-subprogram.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..d817520
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/344-powerpc32-bpf-fetch-fifth-argument-in-BPF-subprogram.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
++From aac99aa8ddbb618839e7e91c75f5a42e6e2ca26b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:31:41 +0100
++Subject: [PATCH 2/4] powerpc32/bpf: fetch fifth argument in BPF subprograms
++
++Commit 7dd0e2848764 ("powerpc/bpf/32: BPF prog is never called with more
++than one arg") removed the BPF_REG_5 load since it's not used for program
++arguments, but that is only true for regular programs - subprograms may
++get up to five arguments, so the load is required there.
++
++Fixes: 7dd0e2848764 ("powerpc/bpf/32: BPF prog is never called with more than one arg")
++Signed-off-by: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++---
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c | 7 +++++++
++ 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+)
++
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
++@@ -161,6 +161,13 @@ void bpf_jit_build_prologue(u32 *image,
++ 		if (bpf_is_seen_register(ctx, i))
++ 			EMIT(PPC_RAW_STW(i, _R1, bpf_jit_stack_offsetof(ctx, i)));
++ 
+++	/* If this is a subprogram, load fifth argument from the stack */
+++	if (ctx->is_subprog && bpf_is_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5))) {
+++		int offset = bpf_has_stack_frame(ctx) ? BPF_PPC_STACKFRAME(ctx) : 0;
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_LWZ(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5) - 1, _R1, offset + 8));
+++		EMIT(PPC_RAW_LWZ(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5), _R1, offset + 12));
+++	}
+++
++ 	/* Setup frame pointer to point to the bpf stack area */
++ 	if (bpf_is_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_FP))) {
++ 		EMIT(PPC_RAW_LI(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_FP) - 1, 0));
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/345-powerpc32-bpf-mark-all-argument-registers-seen-in-fu.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/345-powerpc32-bpf-mark-all-argument-registers-seen-in-fu.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..60a7f22
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/345-powerpc32-bpf-mark-all-argument-registers-seen-in-fu.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,69 @@
++From 63cc0b48408b5f310293162cf171af546079e902 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 15:33:37 +0100
++Subject: [PATCH 3/4] powerpc32/bpf: mark all argument registers seen in
++ function calls
++
++The JIT code tracks which registers are used and remaps based on that,
++but currently does not account for the fact that function calls implicitly
++use registers because the called function will treat them as arguments.
++
++Therefore, if a function merely passes through one of its arguments
++to another function call, it will incorrectly be considered unused and may
++be overwritten with another register's contents.
++
++In practice, this is unlikely to affect main programs as they only get
++one argument (the context, which they are very likely to touch somehow
++in addition to passing it on), so they'll have to touch other registers
++if they want to pass them as arguments. Subprograms, however, are much
++likelier to hit this.
++
++Similarly, tail calls pass R1 to the callee, so mark it used as well.
++
++Fixes: 51c66ad849a7 ("powerpc/bpf: Implement extended BPF on PPC32")
++Fixes: a8936569a07b ("powerpc/bpf: Always reallocate BPF_REG_5, BPF_REG_AX and TMP_REG when possible")
++Signed-off-by: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++---
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c | 17 ++++++++++++++++-
++ 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
++
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
++@@ -298,7 +298,7 @@ int bpf_jit_build_body(struct bpf_prog *
++ {
++ 	const struct bpf_insn *insn = fp->insnsi;
++ 	int flen = fp->len;
++-	int i, ret;
+++	int i, ret, max_arg;
++ 
++ 	/* Start of epilogue code - will only be valid 2nd pass onwards */
++ 	u32 exit_addr = addrs[flen];
++@@ -1075,6 +1075,18 @@ int bpf_jit_build_body(struct bpf_prog *
++ 			if (ret < 0)
++ 				return ret;
++ 
+++			/*
+++			 * We don't know which arguments the called function will use,
+++			 * so if this is a subprogram, assume the worst case where it
+++			 * uses all of them. Regular programs only get one argument,
+++			 * so any others would have been explicitly set.
+++			 */
+++			max_arg = ctx->is_subprog ? BPF_REG_5 : BPF_REG_1;
+++			for (int reg = BPF_REG_1; reg <= max_arg; ++reg) {
+++				bpf_set_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(reg) - 1);
+++				bpf_set_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(reg));
+++			}
+++
++ 			if (bpf_is_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5))) {
++ 				EMIT(PPC_RAW_STW(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5) - 1, _R1, 8));
++ 				EMIT(PPC_RAW_STW(bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_5), _R1, 12));
++@@ -1301,6 +1313,9 @@ int bpf_jit_build_body(struct bpf_prog *
++ 		 * Tail call
++ 		 */
++ 		case BPF_JMP | BPF_TAIL_CALL:
+++			bpf_set_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1) - 1);
+++			bpf_set_seen_register(ctx, bpf_to_ppc(BPF_REG_1));
+++
++ 			ctx->seen |= SEEN_TAILCALL;
++ 			ret = bpf_jit_emit_tail_call(image, ctx, addrs[i + 1]);
++ 			if (ret < 0)
+diff --git a/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/346-powerpc32-bpf-don-t-fold-MOV-into-instructions-that-.patch b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/346-powerpc32-bpf-don-t-fold-MOV-into-instructions-that-.patch
+new file mode 100644
+index 0000000..c730efb
+--- /dev/null
++++ b/target/linux/generic/pending-6.12/346-powerpc32-bpf-don-t-fold-MOV-into-instructions-that-.patch
+@@ -0,0 +1,40 @@
++From 60c29b687fcc54ca8e56c05d0088550c8b880dd3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:38:47 +0100
++Subject: [PATCH 4/4] powerpc32/bpf: don't fold MOV into instructions that use
++ dst_reg
++
++The PPC32 eBPF JIT folds MOVs followed by an ALU operation to the same
++destination into 3-operand PPC instructions by dropping the MOV and
++reusing its source as the second operand for the second instruction.
++
++That is wrong when the second instruction also uses the destination
++register as its source, since the MOV that would have assigned it
++is dropped, so the folded instruction will end up using the previous
++content of the register.
++
++For example, "r2 = r1; r2 += r2" gets folded to "r2 = r1 + r2", while
++the correct result would be 2 * r1.
++
++In particular, this also happens for zero-extension MOVs inserted by the
++verifier, where "r2 = r1; w2 = w2" becomes just "w2 = w2", so the actual
++assignment is skipped.
++
++Fixes: 19daf0aef84f ("powerpc/bpf/32: perform three operands ALU operations")
++Signed-off-by: Lukas Stockner <lukas@lukasstockner.de>
++---
++ arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c | 3 ++-
++ 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
++
++--- a/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
+++++ b/arch/powerpc/net/bpf_jit_comp32.c
++@@ -327,7 +327,8 @@ int bpf_jit_build_body(struct bpf_prog *
++ 		if (i && (BPF_CLASS(code) == BPF_ALU64 || BPF_CLASS(code) == BPF_ALU) &&
++ 		    (BPF_CLASS(prevcode) == BPF_ALU64 || BPF_CLASS(prevcode) == BPF_ALU) &&
++ 		    BPF_OP(prevcode) == BPF_MOV && BPF_SRC(prevcode) == BPF_X &&
++-		    insn[i - 1].dst_reg == insn[i].dst_reg && insn[i - 1].imm != 1) {
+++		    insn[i - 1].dst_reg == insn[i].dst_reg && insn[i - 1].imm != 1 &&
+++		    !(BPF_SRC(code) == BPF_X && insn[i].src_reg == insn[i].dst_reg)) {
++ 			src2_reg = bpf_to_ppc(insn[i - 1].src_reg);
++ 			src2_reg_h = src2_reg - 1;
++ 			ctx->idx = addrs[i - 1] / 4;
+-- 
+2.56.1
+
